00:00:03.919 --> 00:00:07.579 align:start position:0%
the furrier cuts out the suede leather  parts a hydraulic press known as a  clicker drives the dyes through the  leather to punch out the pieces the  largest one will be used for the bottom  and sides of the moccasins it's called  the foot  the other parts are a sole to reinforce  the bottom and the vamp which is the  upper part of the slipper  [Music]  next the fire cuts out pieces of thick  polyester fleece which will be used to  create plush liners he arranges the  moccasin components in the correct  sequence for sewing this is called a  layup and it helps the sewing process go  faster  [Music]  she sews beaded designs into this  moccasins upper  [Music]  these geometric designs are a nod to the  past but their purpose is purely  ornamental not spiritual after lining  the beaded vamp with fleece the worker  stitches a leather strip around the  edges creating a border known as a welt  the Walt gives the vamp a more  attractive finish - so the vamp - the  rest of the moccasin she uses a special  sewing machine it pleats the top of the  foot to fit the vamp as it stitches  [Music]  the moccasins are now structurally  complete and they're ready for the fur  trim  the worker stitches the rabbit fur  strips together to create one long strip  that will fit to the moccasin the length  of the strip will vary depending on the  size of the moccasin she makes an  initial chain stitch and then doubles  back to lock the stitch down in order to  prevent it from unraveling  she aligns the fur trim with the collar  of the moccasin and sews it to it  [Music]  after stitching the worker leaves the  fur tucked down into the moccasin  this makes it easier to apply glue  around the top of the moccasin so that  when the fur is folded up and over it it  stays in place with that this modern-day  version of traditional indigenous  Footwear is ready for its first steps  [Music]
 

